crier keygen — ed25519 keypair for agent "frank"

  key file          : /tmp/TestRunDispatchesKeygenWithoutStartingAServer3899209378/001/frank.key  (PKCS#8 PEM, mode 0600)
  agent id          : frank
  public key (hex)  : bfb1410d61ed96a19d4ea63ce885f8ff030908fea4d6105b4a8679eddb495c79
  server            : http://localhost:8767

  verified          : re-read the file from disk, parsed it with the server's own key loader, and
                      signed + verified a sample payload with it — PASS.
                      The public key above is the file's key, and this is exactly what the server
                      does to every signed request. No openssl and no xxd were used.

Registration — POST /agents takes exactly this body (the clients below send it for you):

  {"id":"frank","public_key":"bfb1410d61ed96a19d4ea63ce885f8ff030908fea4d6105b4a8679eddb495c79"}

Next, with no openssl and no hex surgery:

  1. start the server (any terminal):
       make run
  2. register + run a full signed round-trip
     (register → deliver → retrieve → ack → publish → subscribe):
       python3 clients/python/round_trip.py --server http://localhost:8767 --id frank --key /tmp/TestRunDispatchesKeygenWithoutStartingAServer3899209378/001/frank.key
       node clients/typescript/round-trip.ts --server http://localhost:8767 --id frank --key /tmp/TestRunDispatchesKeygenWithoutStartingAServer3899209378/001/frank.key
  3. or in your own code:
       from crier_client import Crier
       c = Crier("http://localhost:8767", agent_id="frank", key_path="/tmp/TestRunDispatchesKeygenWithoutStartingAServer3899209378/001/frank.key")
       c.register()                     # POST /agents with the public key above
       c.deliver("bob", {"hello": "world"})  # bob needs no key of his own to RECEIVE
       for m in c.retrieve(): ...       # signed retrieve; c.ack(m) when handled

  Keep the key file secret — the server only ever needs the public key.
